Question: IEnumerable vs IQueryable C#:
Answer:
- Both are Interface for .Net collection and IQueryble inherites from IEnumerable means whatever things IEnumerable do
that IQueryble also do. but somethings that Iquerable do but IEnumerable not.
- If Use IEnumerable its fetch and give all the data from the table to the client. then the filter is getting applied.
  means when use IEnumerable and apply filter on it . The full data go from sql server to the clientside then the filter is applied.
- IQuerable sent only required record to client. after apply filter.
- IEnumerable filter logic executed on client side, and IQueryble filter logic executed on DB Side by using sql.
- IEnumerable is in-memory collection and IQueryabl is collection connected to DB via EF or Linq. 
- If you working in-memory collection that no connection to RDBMS then better to use IEnumerable.
       And
  when working to collection that connected to DB thend IQuerable is use, because it reduce network traffic.and also utilse the power of SQL


example: 
static void Main(string[] args)
{
    Test123Entities dal = new Test123Entities();
    IEnumerable<Employee> ob = dal.Employees;  // EF loading empolyee table
    IEnumerable<Employee> d = ob.Where(x => x.Empid == 2).ToList<Employee>();// filter
}

