Question: Middleware and filter difference
answar: 
The Big Picture
. Middleware is the outer shell of your application. It handles every single request that comes into your server.

. Filters are the inner workers. They only run when a request successfully reaches an MVC Controller or Web API Action.

The Airport Analogy
. Middleware is like the Airport Security & Baggage Check. Everyone who enters the airport must go through it. 
   It doesn't matter what airline you are flying or where you are going.

. Filters are like the Gate Agent. They only check you right before you board your specific flight (the Controller/Action). 
  If you aren't taking a flight (e.g., you just came to the airport to pick someone up), you never see the gate agent.

1. Middleware
  Middleware is a chain of components that form the request pipeline. Every request passes through them in the order they are registered.

Scope: Global (Application level). 
 Context: Has access to HttpContext (Headers, Cookies, raw Request/Response body). 
 When it runs: Very early. Before ASP.NET Core even knows which Controller or Action will handle the request.
 What it can do: Modify headers, log raw requests, handle CORS, serve static files, or completely short-circuit the request (e.g., return a 401 Unauthorized and stop).
 Use Cases: Global Exception Handling, CORS, Authentication (setting the User identity), Static File Serving, Rate Limiting.

2. Filters
Filters are attributes or classes that run inside the MVC/Web API pipeline. They wrap around the execution of a specific action method.

Scope: MVC/Controller/Action level (can be applied globally to all controllers, to a specific controller, or to a single action).
Context: Has access to ActionExecutingContext / ActionExecutedContext. It knows about the Action Arguments, Model State, and the specific Controller/Action being executed.
When it runs: Later. After routing has happened and the framework has selected the Controller and Action.
What it can do: Modify the model before it enters the action, short-circuit the action (e.g., if validation fails), or modify the result after the action finishes

========= Types of Filters:

. Authorization Filters: Run first (e.g., [Authorize]).

. Resource Filters: Run after authorization, before model binding.

. Action Filters: Run before and after the action method (great for logging).

. Exception Filters: Catch errors thrown in the action.

. Result Filters: Run before and after the result is formatted (e.g., JSON).

Key Differences (Side-by-Side)

Feature	                                Middleware	                                                  Filters
Level                         	  Application level	                                             MVC / Controller / Action level
. Runs for static files?	        Yes (e.g., .css, .jpg, .html)	                                 No (Only runs for MVC endpoints)
. Access to Route Data?	          Limited (Routing middleware handles this)	                     Yes (Knows exactly which Controller/Action is executing)
. Access to Model/Action          No                                                             Yes
   Arguments?	          
. Execution Order	               Registered in Program.cs. Runs outside-in.                    	Runs inside MVC. Order is strictly defined
                                                                                               (Auth -> Resource -> Action -> Exception -> Result).
. Typical Use Case	             Global CORS, HSTS, Static Files, Authentication              	Model validation, Action logging, modifying JSON output

=== Summary Rule of Thumb
- If you need to do something to every single request (even requests for images or text files) -> Use Middleware.

- If you need to do something specifically related to your Controller's logic 
(like checking if a user's input is valid before running the action) -> Use a Filter.

