Question: Design Pattern
1. Dependency Injection (DI) Pattern
2. Repository Pattern

3. Builder Pattern
Category: Creational
What it is: Used to construct complex objects step-by-step. It separates the construction of an object from its representation.
Why it's used: When an object has many optional parameters, constructors become messy. The Builder pattern makes it readable.
How it's implemented in ASP.NET Core:
You use this every single time you start an ASP.NET Core app! Look at your Program.cs:

4. Chain of Responsibility Pattern (Middleware)
Category: Behavioral
What it is: A chain of handler objects. A request is passed along the chain. Each handler decides either to process the request or pass it to the next handler.

Why it's used: It decouples the sender of a request from its receivers.

How it's implemented in ASP.NET Core:
The entire Middleware pipeline is the Chain of Responsibility pattern.


============= Summary of what a typical project uses:
- DI to wire everything together.

- Repository to hide Entity Framework from the controllers.

- Builder (via WebApplication.CreateBuilder) to configure the app.

- Chain of Responsibility (via Middleware) to process HTTP requests.

- Singleton (via AddSingleton) for caching or app-wide configuration settings.

