Question: Map , run , use difference
In ASP.NET Core, app.Use(), app.Run(), and app.Map() are the three fundamental methods used to configure the middleware pipeline.
You can think of the pipeline as a series of gates that every HTTP request must pass through. 
Each method serves a distinct purpose in how a request flows through these gates.

⚙️ app.Use(): The Chain Builder
app.Use() is used to add a middleware component that can perform some logic and then choose whether to pass the request
to the next middleware in the pipeline.

Core Characteristic: It "chains" the pipeline. It receives a next parameter, which represents the subsequent delegate.

Behavior: You can execute code both before and after calling next().

Short-Circuiting: If you do not call next(), the request stops there, and no further middleware is executed.

Use Case: This is the most flexible method, used for cross-cutting concerns like logging, authentication, 
and custom request processing where you might want to pass the request along.


🛑 app.Run(): The Terminator
app.Run() adds a terminal middleware. It is the end of the line.

Core Characteristic: It does not have a next parameter.

Behavior: It processes the request and generates a response, completely stopping the pipeline. Any middleware registered after a Run delegate will never be executed.

Convention: Run is a convention used for the final piece of logic in your pipeline. Typically, an application will have only one Run method, placed at the very end.

Use Case: It's perfect for a "catch-all" or fallback handler when no other middleware or endpoint has handled the request.


🌿 app.Map(): The Branch Router
app.Map() is used to create a branch in the pipeline based on the request's path.

Core Characteristic: It conditionally branches the pipeline. If the incoming request path starts with the specified path,
a separate, isolated pipeline is executed.

Behavior: The branching is based on path matching. It's not a middleware that gets added to the main chain but rather a fork in the road.

Path Modification: When Map is used, the matched path segment is removed from HttpRequest.Path and added to HttpRequest.PathBase.

Use Case: It's ideal for isolating specific parts of your application, such as handling requests for /api or /admin differently, or for setting up separate health check endpoints.



💡 Bonus: UseMiddleware<T>() and MapWhen()
You may also encounter two related methods:

app.UseMiddleware<MyMiddleware>(): This is the strongly-typed, convention-based way to add a custom middleware class. 
It offers full Dependency Injection (DI) support in the middleware's constructor, making it the preferred approach for complex, 
reusable middleware components.

app.MapWhen(): This is a more powerful version of Map. Instead of matching on a URL path, 
it branches the pipeline based on the result of a predicate (e.g., checking for a specific header, query string, or any other logic).

